da  and it made me realize that if you walk  into an environment where you are  surrounded by  this tremendous assortment this  tremendous choice of things that you  might want to acquire  it encourages you to want to acquire  more and i think that's part of the  psychology behind these stores  the home depot  in the u.s or costco or any of these big  box retailers they put so much stuff in  front of you that our natural  inclination is to consume more  i think something like that happens to  us as language learners when we are at  the stage where we have yet to master  our first foreign language  we feel that it's very difficult we'll  never get there we we're overly critical  of our own uh  results and achievements uh we don't  have that  confidence that we're gonna get there  and and of course that's very very  important as i said before if i'm  looking for something at home and i know  it's at home  i'm going to look for it until i find it  but if i think it might be at the office  or it might be somewhere else i'm not  quite as determined when looking for it  because i'm not so sure that that it's  to be found at home and so for that  first language we're not so sure that  we're going to achieve the level that we  want so at some level we don't try as  hard as we should once we've been  through one  foreign language and a second and a  third we're now fully confident that we  can do it so now it's just a matter of  allocating our time and now  language learning itself becomes more  addictive because we've had that sense  of satisfaction of achieving success  all these languages become very  attractive to us we see now like if  you're at link you can look at the drop  list there's 21 languages and they've  all got content there to listen to and  read and gee you know i want to do them  all and  and so it's very easy to be distracted  as is generally the case today with  anything having to do with the internet  there's so much choice there  that it's very difficult at times to  stay on task  a couple of things about multiple  languages personally i prefer to focus  on one new language at a time because it  requires so much dedication and  concentration  however as i've said before i can dabble  or refresh another language where i'm  fairly well along but that would be the  maximum two languages like now i've  decided i'm going to do korea  still contain continue to do my  ukrainian but i'm not going to do polish  for now even though  again it was tempting to do polish  because the vocabulary is very similar  to ukrainian and now that i've done  czech and russian ukrainian i want to  get in in the polish so i had to make a  decision i'm going to be in korea in  march  i should really get the level of my  korean up so that's going to be my focus  as well as listening to ukrainian and  some extent russian as i follow events  taking place they're more out of  interest in those events than as a  deliberate language learning focus my  language learning focus is going to move  to korean  okay so  first point is focus on one at a time at  least 80 of your effort that is my  preference now i want to be very clear  in all this there are other people who  are happy learning five languages at the  same time but if i'm asked what i like  to do i prefer to focus on one at the  time 80 percent  the second thing is you can focus on one  for three months and then move to  another  and one of the surprising things that i  have found and i've had it confirmed by  many other people  if you leave a language for three months  six months  and then go back to it again  you end up being better  okay you are initially rusty but as soon  as you  get back into the swing of that language  you are better  now i used to explain this to myself  in terms of a  sort of uh something that i put into the  oven that's still baking  and so that uh you know i  noticed on many occasions i would for  example  i was at one point listening to a lot of  chinese at home  then i had to go to sweden so i focused  on swedish and when i came back to my  chinese i understood it better  and  i thought somehow that that learning  effort continued to gestate in the brain  somehow  but i don't think that's what it is i  think it's more i provided a link  earlier to a video by a professor bjork  who talked about interleaving and  the importance of learning and  forgetting and re-learning and  re-forgetting and re-learning that that  process  is much more powerful  than  staying with  one set of information you know trying  to nail something like we often do for  an exam  that that in effect we may in fact  retain it for the exam but it won't stay  with us whereas things that we forget  relearn forget relearn  we  are able to learn those better and it  might just be that that's the  explanation so if you were to go three  months on language a and then leave it  and then do three months on language b  when you come back to language a  again with the intention of focusing on  it  uh you will be initially a bit rusty but  as you recover to where you were you'll  end up stronger understand better better  able to express yourself so  while i i  recommend at least based on my own  experience i have a preference for  focusing on one language as long as i'm  sort of  motivated most motivated to focus on  that language i know that all the  languages that i leave like my czech  like my romanian like my ukrainian even  the little bit that i did in polish  whatever i did there is not lost and  whenever i choose to pick that up again  it'll all be still in my brain and as i  revive that as i relearn it from  wherever it's  disappeared as i start to recall it and  relearn it again i will know it better  than i knew it before so the answer is  insofar as my own learning habits are  concerned i prefer to focus on one  language at a time but i'm not afraid to  put the language aside and focus on  another language for a while because i  know that the effort that i have put  into  that language that i've put aside is not  wasted and when i go back to that  language i will  after that initial sort of rusty period  i will be stronger  than i was before so i hope that perhaps  a little bit confusing but i hope that  answers uh your question and i do  appreciate questions and comments and if  i'm able to respond to them and use them  as uh content for my video i'm i'm only  too happy to do so thank you for  listening bye for now
 

